      "side": 1,
      "message": "You\u0027ve hit the most awkward aspect of the libfdt wrapper:\n```\nerror[E0505]: cannot move out of `cur` because it is borrowed\n   --\u003e packages/modules/Virtualization/pvmfw/src/fdt.rs:679:13\n    |\n664 |         next \u003d if let Some(mut cur) \u003d next {\n    |                            ------- binding `cur` declared here\n665 |             let mut next_subnode \u003d cur.first_subnode()?;\n    |                                    --- borrow of `cur` occurs here\n...\n679 |             cur.next_compatible(opp_compat)?\n    |             ^^^\n    |             |\n    |             move out of `cur` occurs here\n    |             borrow later used here\n\nerror: aborting due to 2 previous errors\n```\nwhich is hard to express in safe Rust because any (mutable) node has the potential to modify the DT as a whole (e.g. by causing a shift during insertions) or other nodes (e.g. NOP-ing a node also NOP-es its subnodes). As a result, our API uses the borrow-checker to prevent multiple simultaneous `FdtNodeMut` existing at the same time.\n\nThis means that the very common pattern:\n```\nfor mut node in ...:\n    for mut subnode in node:\n        # modify subnode\n```\ncan\u0027t work (because `node` and `subnode` exist at the same time).",
